64927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer-field/choices ["q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98524"] :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-98524/selection/ma-v1/d776997a0945a28ae952f70c339b1b567f1d731d0e0b8ee5c4f0d8cc555ccf5d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c7817704-2c2c-5c82-b726-44f05a57b617" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "d46e0d50-1dfa-5ae7-a1f4-0e084a87d49a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "4116bad4-7704-5d3c-aead-f4e88e539e60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "600d6f02-be35-5d88-88d5-edbe61169d3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "03deeae6-3174-507a-874c-fa238c78bfd9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0197e2d-78e4-59b1-866d-0326af420662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99827/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-99827/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-99827/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-99827/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-99827/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-99827/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99827" :question/id #uuid "cf274c2e-03ff-5221-83a7-3ad5b6f8168e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99827/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e085f337-a139-5495-a885-f22a8b50d1d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "532611c8-25ed-5d0e-b24b-ff6b277154b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "c87e8ada-6876-5f3b-aef3-f4132922cfb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "bbaa87ef-d443-57f2-b170-104896a37179" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d15a36c7-be51-5acc-aa89-16a7cb924587" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca5951-ba3c-5cda-bd2b-1de4dbbfd8d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99848/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-99848/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-99848/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-99848/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-99848/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-99848/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99848" :question/id #uuid "14911b6b-4e1d-55bf-aff2-fa02f5f045e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99848/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99848"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "f3edf2dc-e079-57e5-96d2-8bce38a9f836" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "2a17dbb6-9043-5b4d-92cb-700ffcbb8d7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1dc2081b-9590-5720-b802-00c42e516f92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193" :answer/id #uuid "612a3ec9-d348-55f6-83af-9b4271e4b630" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1385ec7d-713a-55c8-92da-323cdfcb2ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4a38bf-1f11-59a4-a2b9-6037f8331898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-100091/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-100091/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-100091/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-100091/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-100091/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193" "q-100091/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100091" :question/id #uuid "07957b89-f6f1-537f-ad10-787ac05c390f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100091" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(1-1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-100091/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100091"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1a5ce868-8739-56b4-bc89-a6ed86321de0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "adc2afe7-960c-51bc-aefd-178846c7b056" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193" :answer/id #uuid "51a3a06c-9bbc-52c5-a0d5-925563e8330a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "5e388b22-5e8d-5396-ae32-978dd6ad14ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "54bdfaeb-dce4-5299-8d37-57c62e3f1c90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5546d092-75d2-5847-909d-f560bed58c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-100119/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-100119/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-100119/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-100119/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193" "q-100119/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-100119/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100119" :question/id #uuid "98763a63-2338-5cf6-99f3-477072c178b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100119" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-100119/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100119"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
